---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';

// PROTOTYPE — these four are MY placeholders, not Hiro's picks. They're real
// djent/prog records purely so the tiles carry realistic name lengths while the
// composition is judged; replace the whole array.
//
// `cover` is a path under public/images/albums/ (square source, 400px+ — they
// render at 115px but retina doubles that). Omit it and the entry falls back to
// a named tile, which is what makes this previewable before any art exists.
// Alt text is "artist — title" per cover: the grid carries no visible captions,
// so alt is the ONLY place the names live for a screen reader.
const albums = [
	{ artist: 'Periphery', title: 'Periphery II' },
	{ artist: 'Meshuggah', title: 'ObZen' },
	{ artist: 'TesseracT', title: 'Altered State' },
	{ artist: 'Animals as Leaders', title: 'The Joy of Motion' },
];
---

<Layout title="About — Hiro Eernisse" description="About Hiro Eernisse, design engineer.">
	<Nav />

	<main class="mx-auto max-w-[960px] px-5 py-12 sm:py-16">
		{/* Side by side again from lg, after one day stacked. The row died on
		    08-02 when the page came in to 960px: the figure held 26rem of that,
		    which left the prose ~346px — about FIVE words a line at the 21.4px
		    it was set in. Both of those numbers moved on Hiro's ask, and the row
		    came back with them. The prose is 19px now and the figure is 19rem,
		    so the text column takes 504px at lg: 26.5 times its own font size,
		    against the 31 that --measure holds. Narrower than the site's reading
		    cap on purpose (Hiro: "narrower"), and 1.6× the width that killed the
		    row. Below lg there is no room for two columns at any of these
		    numbers, so it still stacks and the prose keeps its own 28rem cap —
		    close enough to the row's 504px that the two states read as one
		    measure rather than two.

		    NOT --measure: that token is the site's reading cap, shared with the
		    home hero and the project pages, and this column is narrower than it
		    for a layout reason that doesn't apply to them. */}
		{/* col-REVERSE below lg (08-03). The row's DOM order is prose-then-photo,
		    which is what the lg layout wants; stacked, that put the page's tallest
		    single element (the photo, 446px at 390px wide) last, so the page ended
		    on an unlabelled block bigger than anything above it. Reversed, the
		    stack opens on the photo the same way the lg row does — the photo is
		    the first thing you see at both widths instead of the first at one and
		    the last at the other.
		    flex-col-reverse rather than order-* on the figure: same result, but the
		    reversal is stated once on the container instead of as a pair of
		    utilities on a child that has to be read against its sibling. Either way
		    the DOM order is unchanged, so the reading order stays greeting → prose
		    → interests → photo.
		    It also puts the reveal cascade back in visual order down the stack: the
		    figure enters at --base 180ms and the column at 700ms, so the photo was
		    already revealing first while sitting last. */}
		<div class="flex flex-col-reverse gap-12 lg:flex-row lg:items-start lg:gap-10">
			<div class="max-w-[28rem] lg:min-w-0 lg:max-w-none lg:flex-1">
				{/* Same size as the section heading below, not the 3xl/4xl page title
				    it was (Hiro, 08-02). The masthead already carries the name at
				    full size on every page, so this h1 is a greeting rather than a
				    title, and at the section heading's size the column reads as two
				    equal blocks under one photo instead of a hero with a footnote.
				    The wave is decorative — aria-hidden, or it reads out as "waving
				    hand" in the middle of a Japanese greeting.

				    mb-4, matching the section heading's mt-4 to its list: the 3rem
				    that used to sit here was cut for a 4xl page title, and at this
				    size it read as a hole rather than a hero's breathing room. Both
				    headings now hold their content at the same distance, and the
				    only big gap left in the column is the 3rem BETWEEN the two
				    blocks — which is the rhythm, tight inside, loose between. */}
				<h1 class="reveal mb-4 text-xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[var(--ink-heading)]" style="--i: 0">
					はじめまして。<span aria-hidden="true">👋</span>
				</h1>
				{/* space-y-5 (23.75px), down from 6 (28.5px) on 08-03. The paragraph
				    break was 0.92× the 30.875px leading and exactly half the 57px
				    section gap — close enough to a section break that the prose read
				    as two blocks rather than one. At 23.75px it is 0.77× the leading
				    (still clearly a paragraph break, since it exceeds the line gap)
				    and 0.42× the section gap, so "tight inside, loose between" is
				    legible as a ratio instead of a factor of two.
				    The h1 keeps mb-4 rather than opening to match: its 19px is
				    deliberately equal to the section headings' mt-4 (see above), and
				    that symmetry is worth more than closing the gap from the other
				    side. */}
				<div class="space-y-5 text-base leading-relaxed">
					<p class="reveal" style="--base: 700ms; --i: 0">I'm a Design Engineer based in the San Francisco Bay Area.</p>
					<p class="reveal" style="--base: 700ms; --i: 1">
						I build with AI, but I make the decisions that matter myself. Taste, and knowing how a
						person moves through a product, is still human work.
					</p>
				</div>
				{/* Started as the bio's closing line — "Outside of work, I play heavy
				    metal drums and hike," a sentence doing a list's job at the end
				    of a paragraph run — and was promoted to a section on 08-02,
				    then filled out to four entries the same day. A real heading, NOT
				    the small-bold uppercase kicker this codebase has dropped twice
				    (see the project page). It takes the reveal slot the removed line
				    held, so the cascade is unchanged.

				    The emojis are decoration on top of words that already say the
				    same thing, so they're aria-hidden: a screen reader would
				    otherwise hear "Playing drums drum".

				    Leading the row (Hiro, 08-02), so each one gets a fixed 1.25em
				    slot rather than riding on its own advance width — that's what
				    puts the LABELS on one left edge, which is the only reason to
				    hang icons off the front of a list. It's a width, not a clip, so a wider glyph
				    later spills into the gap instead of losing a pixel. */}
				<section class="reveal mt-12" style="--base: 700ms; --i: 2">
					<h2 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[var(--ink-heading)]">Personal Interests</h2>
					<ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-base leading-relaxed">
						<li class="flex items-baseline gap-2">
							<span aria-hidden="true" class="w-[1.25em] shrink-0">🥁</span>
							<span>Playing drums</span>
						</li>
						<li class="flex items-baseline gap-2">
							<span aria-hidden="true" class="w-[1.25em] shrink-0">👾</span>
							<span>Video game design &amp; pixel art</span>
						</li>
						<li class="flex items-baseline gap-2">
							<span aria-hidden="true" class="w-[1.25em] shrink-0">🎬</span>
							<span>Tokusatsu (Godzilla, Ultraman, etc.)</span>
						</li>
						<li class="flex items-baseline gap-2">
							<span aria-hidden="true" class="w-[1.25em] shrink-0">🥾</span>
							<span>Enjoying nature and hiking</span>
						</li>
					</ul>
				</section>
			</div>
			{/* Fixed 19rem at lg so the flex-1 column above resolves to a known
			    width (see the note there); it is the last thing in the row, so
			    shrink-0 keeps the prose taking the slack rather than the photo.
			    Below lg the row is gone and the old stacked caps stand. */}
			<figure class="reveal w-full max-w-[18rem] sm:max-w-[22rem] lg:w-[19rem] lg:max-w-none lg:shrink-0" style="--base: 180ms; --i: 0">
				<img
					src="/images/me.webp"
					alt="Hiro Eernisse"
					width="832"
					height="1040"
					class="aspect-[4/5] w-full rounded-[1.5px] border border-[var(--line)] object-cover"
				/>
				<figcaption class="mt-2.5 flex items-center gap-1.5 text-xs text-[var(--ink-soft)]">
					<svg
						class="h-3.5 w-3.5 shrink-0"
						viewBox="0 0 24 24"
						fill="none"
						stroke="currentColor"
						stroke-width="2"
						stroke-linecap="square"
						stroke-linejoin="miter"
						aria-hidden="true"
					>
						<path d="M12 22s7-6.5 7-12a7 7 0 0 0-14 0c0 5.5 7 12 7 12Z" />
						<circle cx="12" cy="10" r="2.5" />
					</svg>
					Eagle Lake, South Lake Tahoe, CA
				</figcaption>
			</figure>
		</div>
			{/* PROTOTYPE (08-02) — covers, not titles. A list of album NAMES
			    under the interests list would have been a second bulleted list
			    reading as more of the first; sleeves are the version that earns the
			    space, because cover art carries taste without a sentence and this
			    becomes the only other image on the page.

			    OUT OF THE PROSE COLUMN AND ACROSS THE PAGE (08-03). It sat inside the
			    504px column, which made the lg row badly bottom-heavy on one side: the
			    text column ran 690px against the figure's 482px, so the whole
			    bottom-right quadrant — everything below the caption, beside this grid
			    — was empty. Lifting this section out takes ~224px off the column, which
			    lands it at ~466px against the photo's 482px: the two columns now end
			    within about 16px of each other and the hole is gone. The row above is
			    still the same two-column arithmetic; this is simply no longer part of it.

			    Four across the full 912.5px: (912.5 − 3 × 14.25) / 4 = 217px a tile,
			    nearly double the 115px it got inside the column and comfortably clear
			    of the ~115px floor where a sleeve stops being recognisable and starts
			    being a swatch. Two-up below sm. Squares on purpose — every sleeve is
			    square, so aspect-square means no art is ever cropped and the row has
			    one flat baseline.

			    mt-12 still, but it is now measured from the ROW's bottom rather than
			    from the interests list, so the 57px reads as the same section break it
			    always was. --i: 3 is unchanged deliberately: this is still the fourth
			    thing to reveal, and the cascade should not notice that it changed
			    parents.

			    Same 1.5px radius and --line border as the photo, so the two image
			    elements read as one material. bg-clip-padding on the fallback tiles:
			    --line is translucent, so without it the tile's own --surface-sunken
			    paints THROUGH the border and the edge lands a different colour than
			    the photo's identical one. */}
			<section class="reveal mt-12" style="--base: 700ms; --i: 3">
				<h2 class="text-xl font-bold tracking-tight text-[var(--ink-heading)]">Favorite albums</h2>
				<ul class="mt-4 grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 sm:grid-cols-4">
					{albums.map((album) => (
						<li>
							{album.cover ? (
								<img
									src={album.cover}
									alt={`${album.artist} — ${album.title}`}
									class="aspect-square w-full rounded-[1.5px] border border-[var(--line)] object-cover"
								/>
							) : (
								<div
									class="flex aspect-square items-end rounded-[1.5px] border border-[var(--line)] bg-[var(--surface-sunken)] bg-clip-padding p-2"
									role="img"
									aria-label={`${album.artist} — ${album.title}`}
								>
									<span aria-hidden="true" class="text-[0.7rem] leading-tight text-[var(--ink-soft)]">
										{album.artist} — {album.title}
									</span>
								</div>
							)}
						</li>
					))}
				</ul>
			</section>
	</main>
</Layout>
